-execdir and -okdir refused to run at all on this target, and the reason is
a path-syntax assumption rather than anything missing.

find's check_path_safety() exists because -execdir runs a command from
inside the directory it is examining, so a $PATH entry that is relative --
"." , "", or "bin" -- would let a directory being searched supply the
program.  GNU find refuses rather than take the risk.  The check splits
$PATH on ':' and calls an entry absolute if it starts with '/'.

Neither half holds here.  NT's PATH separator is ';', which it has to be on
a system where every absolute path contains a ':' after the drive letter,
and ntlibc's execvp splits it that way -- src/process/find_program.c says
so in as many words, and its own path search would therefore look in
entirely different places from the ones find is vetting.  Measured before
this patch was written: with wine's ordinary environment,

    find.exe a -name '*.txt' -execdir echo {} ';'

exits with

    find.exe: The relative path 'C' is included in the PATH environment
    variable, which is insecure in combination with the -execdir action of
    find.  Please remove that entry from $PATH

-- the 'C' being the drive letter of C:\windows\system32, cut off at the
colon.  Every $PATH on this platform fails that way, so the feature was not
degraded, it was unreachable.

The patch is guarded by NT_PATH_SYNTAX, defined in config.h, rather than by
_WIN32: this build compiles with -U_WIN32 on purpose (gnulib reads _WIN32 as
"the Microsoft C runtime is underneath", which is not true here), so _WIN32
is not available to key a decision on, and a decision about path SYNTAX is
in any case a different question from which C runtime is underneath.

The security property the check exists to protect is preserved, not waived:
after the patch a relative entry -- "bin", "." or an empty field -- is still
rejected, and so is a bare "C:", which on NT means "the current directory on
drive C" and is exactly the case the check is about.  What is accepted is a
leading separator or a drive letter followed by one.

--- find/parser.c
+++ find/parser.c
@@ -2716,7 +2716,17 @@
 check_path_safety (const char *action)
 {
   const char *path = getenv ("PATH");
+#ifdef NT_PATH_SYNTAX
+  /* NT's PATH is ';'-separated, and ntlibc's execvp splits it that way
+     (src/process/find_program.c).  ':' cannot be the separator on a system
+     whose absolute paths begin "C:", and splitting on it here made every
+     ordinary $PATH look like a list of one-letter RELATIVE directories --
+     so -execdir and -okdir refused to run at all.  See
+     patches/nt-execdir-path-syntax.patch.  */
+  const char *path_separators = ";";
+#else
   const char *path_separators = ":";
+#endif
   size_t pos, len;
 
   if (NULL == path)
@@ -2744,7 +2754,17 @@
 		   "or leading or trailing colons)"),
 		 action);
 	}
+#ifdef NT_PATH_SYNTAX
+      /* Absolute on this target means a leading separator OR a drive
+	 letter followed by one -- "C:\\windows" is not a relative path.  A
+	 bare "C:" IS relative (it means the current directory on drive C),
+	 and is correctly rejected here.  */
+      else if (! (path[pos] == '/' || path[pos] == '\\'
+		  || (2 < len && path[pos + 1] == ':'
+		      && (path[pos + 2] == '/' || path[pos + 2] == '\\'))))
+#else
       else if (path[pos] != '/')
+#endif
 	{
 	  char *relpath = strndup (&path[pos], len);
 	  error (EXIT_FAILURE, 0,
